---
source: tests/steps/l01_03_lexer_integers/mod.rs
expression: "out.join(\"\\n\")"
---
timeout 200ms    [8..13] the number `200` runs into the name `ms` with nothing between them, and a number must not run into a name. Put an operator or a space between them, as in `200 * ms`.
count 1__000     [6..12] the number `1__000` has a `_` that does not stand between two digits, and `_` may stand only between two digits, as in `1_000_000`. Remove that `_`.
count 1_000_     [6..12] the number `1_000_` has a `_` that does not stand between two digits, and `_` may stand only between two digits, as in `1_000_000`. Remove that `_`.
mask 0b1012      [5..11] the number `0b1012` starts with `0b`, whose digits are 0 and 1, and `2` is not one of them. Write the number with those digits, or in decimal without `0b`.
mode 0o789       [5..10] the number `0o789` starts with `0o`, whose digits are 0 to 7, and `8` is not one of them. Write the number with those digits, or in decimal without `0o`.
color 0x         [6..8] `0x` starts a number in base 16, and no digit of base 16 follows it. Write the digits after `0x`, as in `0x1F`.
color 0xG0       [6..10] `0x` starts a number in base 16, and no digit of base 16 follows it. Write the digits after `0x`, as in `0x1F`.
big 0X1F         [4..8] `0X` is written with a capital `X`, and a number's base is marked with a small letter: `0x`, `0o` or `0b`. Write `0x`.
flags 0b_101     [6..12] `0b` starts a number in base 2, and no digit of base 2 follows it. Write the digits after `0b`, as in `0b101`.
day 1d           [4..6] the number `1` runs into the name `d` with nothing between them, and a number must not run into a name. Put an operator or a space between them, as in `1 * d`.
